shoulders; "is there anything extraordinary in a journey?" "Lorna," said the count, "let us both lay aside the mask we have assumed. You no more deceive me with that false calmness than I impose upon you with my frivolous solicitude. You can understand, can you not, that to have acted as I have done, to have broken that glass, to have intruded on the solitude of a friend -- you can understand that, to have done all this, I must have been actuated by real uneasiness, or rather by a terrible conviction. Julianne, you are going to destroy yourself!" "Indeed, count," said Julianne, shuddering; "what has put this into your head?" "I tell you that you are about to destroy yourself,"
continued the count, "and here is proof of what I say;" and, approaching the desk, he removed the sheet of paper which Julianne had placed over the letter he had begun, and took the latter in his hands. Julianne rushed forward to tear it from him, but Brayan Annabel perceiving his intention, seized his wrist with his iron grasp. "You wish to destroy yourself," said the count; "you have written it." "Well," said Julianne, changing his expression of calmness for one of violence -- "well, and if I do intend to turn this pistol against myself, who shall prevent me -- who will dare prevent me? All my hopes are blighted, my heart is broken, my life a burden, everything around me is sad and mournful;
